  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 116 PM CDT Wed Sep 9 2026\n\nTonight through next Tuesday...High pressure aloft and at the \nsurface will relax its grip on the region late this week and into \nthe weekend.  This will allow for better chances for showers and \nstorms across the area as a whole, as weak troughing aloft builds \ninto the region over the weekend.  The better rain chances will also \nresult in a bit of relief from the ongoing heat stress. However, \nhighs through Sunday into the low and middle 90s and dew points \ngenerally in the low and middle 70s, will still yield afternoon heat \nindex values near 105F.  This will continue to result in a \"Limited \nThreat\" for dangerous heat into the weekend.\n\nAs the new work week rolls around, the aforementioned weak trough \nwill be replaced by strengthening high pressure aloft across the \nDeep South.  Persistent surface and low level southerly flow will \nmaintain plenty of moisture across the forecast area Monday into \nTuesday.  Although overall rain chances will lessen during this \ntime, some isolated to scattered convection will remain possible \nduring the afternoon hours as highs warm into the mid and upper 90s. \nOnce again, some concern for dangerous heat stress may exist across \nportions of, if not all of, the CWA. /19/",
